⏺ Council verdict — answering each question with the evidence I gathered, sized
  to the question.

  ---
  Q1 — Rename Xcode project/target?

  Verdict: No, don't rename the target. Yes, share + rename the scheme.

  Evidence:
  - ios/App/App.xcodeproj with target App is the Capacitor 8 iOS template's
  hardcoded layout. npx cap sync ios walks that path by name.
  - xcshareddata/xcschemes/ is empty — Xcode is auto-generating an unshared
  scheme from the target name "App".
  - capacitor.config.ts already has appName: 'Hexmind' and bundle id
  app.hexmind.ios is set on the target. The on-device springboard already says
  "Hexmind" (since 85d2e9d).
  - Apple signing + App Store Connect archives are keyed by bundle id, not
  target name. Renaming the target wouldn't break those.
  - What WOULD break on a full rename: derived data path, the ios/App/App
  directory layout that Capacitor walks, possibly Podfile/SPM target refs in
  project.pbxproj, anything you've muscle-memorized about the path.

  Recommendation: in Xcode → Product → Scheme → Manage Schemes → rename "App" to
   "Hexmind", tick Shared. Commits a xcshareddata/xcschemes/Hexmind.xcscheme
  file. Cosmetic win, zero Capacitor risk. The "App" target name stays — that's
  an internal label nobody sees outside Xcode.

  A full target rename is high-effort, Capacitor-fragile, and zero user-visible
  benefit (the App Store listing, springboard label, and bundle id all already
  say Hexmind). Skip.

  ---
  Q2 — Single-page Settings layout

  Verdict: Drop the tab switcher. One scrolling page, three sectioned groups.

  Proposed grouping (top to bottom on a phone modal):

  ┌─ Inference ─────────────────────────┐
  │  [Apple Intelligence status card]   │
  │  Creativity   ◯───●─────  60%       │
  │  Cross-Pollination ●─────  20%      │
  │  Smart Expansion           [● on]   │
  ├─ Display ───────────────────────────┤
  │  Theme              [Light | Dark]  │
  │  Font Size       [S][M][L][XL]      │
  │  Animations                [● on]   │
  ├─ Data ──────────────────────────────┤
  │  Auto-Save                 [● on]   │
  └─────────────────────────────────────┘

  Inference up top (most-touched), Data at the bottom (set-once). Section
  dividers are cheap; the tab switcher costs a tap and hides controls people
  forget exist.

  ---
  Q3a — Does FM honor temperature?

  Verdict: Yes, real. Slider is honest.

  Evidence from the Swift plugin
  (ios/App/App/FoundationModelsPlugin.swift:76-79):

  let options = GenerationOptions(
      temperature: temperature,
      maximumResponseTokens: maxTokens
  )
  let response = try await session.respond(to: prompt, options: options)

  Apple's GenerationOptions accepts temperature in the 0.0-2.0 range — low
  values stable/predictable, high values creative. Confirmed by Apple's
  developer docs and the WWDC25 deep-dive talk. Our app sends 0.7-1.3, which is
  real and acted on.

  So your creativity slider is honest. Both layers contribute:
  - Temperature (real, server-side) — sampling stochasticity on the model
  - Prompt phrasing ("Logical, concrete and safe" / "Wild, abstract and
  out-of-the-box") — instruction-level steering

  If you want to level up the slider's range, you have headroom: Apple supports
  up to 2.0; you're capped at 1.3. Bumping to ~1.6 at max would give "Creative"
  mode noticeably more variance.

  ---
  Q3b — Smart Expansion toggle wiring

  Verdict: Toggle works, but there's a paper bug.

  End-to-end trace:

  Where: HexmindApp.tsx:131 — useState(true) for enableSmartExpansion
  Line: ✅
  Status:
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Where: HexmindApp.tsx:163 — useAIGeneration({ enableSmartExpansion: true })
  Line: ⚠️ hardcoded true, ignores state
  Status:
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Where: HexmindApp.tsx:783-789 — local generateNeighbors gates
    nodesToAutoExpand.push(newNode) on the state var
  Line: ✅
  Status:
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Where: HexmindApp.tsx:805 — second gate before triggering expansion
  Line: ✅
  Status:
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Where: SettingsModal.tsx:367-381 — Switch wired to state setter
  Line: ✅
  Status:

  The user-visible behavior is correctly gated because the local
  generateNeighbors is the primary tile-tap path (the hook's generateNeighbors
  is shadowed — same pattern as the pre-strip bug from prior session lesson #7).
   The hook's enableSmartExpansion: true is a paper bug — it's a prop the hook
  receives but doesn't actually act on for autoExpand triggering, so the
  hardcoded true is harmless today.

  Still worth fixing for hygiene: change line 163 to enableSmartExpansion: 
  enableSmartExpansion. Costs one line; closes the shadow-bug attack surface if
  anyone wires up the hook's autoExpand path later.

  ---
  Dissent / what I'd flag

  The shadowing pattern is back. There are still two generateNeighbors (hook +
  local), and the local one is the one that runs. This is the exact
  configuration that caused the pre-strip "tile-tap never fires FM" bug. Worth a
   follow-up commit to either delete the hook's version or have HexmindApp.tsx
  delegate to it. Out of scope for this round but on the radar.

  Don't ship more controls than you'll keep. Cross-Pollination is plumbed
  (slider exists, bridgingIntensity modulates the prompt) but it's not obvious
  it changes user-visible output strongly. If TestFlight users don't touch it,
  kill it before the App Store submission rather than carrying dead UX.

  ---
  Action plan, ordered

  1. (2 min) Xcode → Manage Schemes → rename + share as "Hexmind". Commit the
  new Hexmind.xcscheme.
  2. (20 min) Refactor SettingsModal.tsx to a single sectioned page. Drop the
  activeTab state and the tab switcher. Keep the iOS info card at the top of the
   Inference section.
  3. (1 line) Fix HexmindApp.tsx:163 — pass the state, not the literal.
  4. (optional, 5 min) Bump max temperature mapping to ~1.6 if you want more
  visible "Creative" mode separation. The Apple cap is 2.0.

  Want me to execute steps 2 + 3 now? Step 1 needs Xcode UI; I can't do it from
  CLI.

  Sources:
  - GenerationOptions | Apple Developer Documentation
  (https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundationmodels/generationoptions)
  - Deep dive into the Foundation Models framework — WWDC25
  (https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2025/301/)
  - Meet the Foundation Models framework — WWDC25
  (https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2025/286/)
  - Exploring the Foundation Models framework — Create with Swift
  (https://www.createwithswift.com/exploring-the-foundation-models-framework/)

