⏺ Cynic returned hard. Key finding: the user-knowledge criterion is the right
  intent, but false negatives are an order of magnitude worse than false 
  positives because the user never knows a question got suppressed — the tree
  just silently grows around the LLM's defaults instead of the user's situation.

  Cynic's specific attack:
  - Apple FoundationModels has aggressive negative-instruction decay; "only ask
  user-knowledge questions" gets crowded out by ~turn 4-5 of running tile-tree
  context.
  - Gemini doesn't decay as much, but it's worse at the meta-classification —
  it'll confidently misclassify factuals as user-knowledge.

  Cynic's proposed alternative — structured field + post-hoc validation:

  LLM emits:
    contextPrompt: "What's your budget?"
    contextPromptJustification: "Cost constraint depends on user's resources"
    userKnowledgeCategory: "constraint"  // preference|constraint|situation|goal

  Validator (cheap regex on justification):
    if justification contains "common", "typically", "standard", "recommended"
      → reject the prompt, convert to branches instead

  Failure mode of rejection = same as not asking. No UX hole. Beats whitelisting
   prefixes (too brittle) and second-LLM-call (too slow on FM).

  The root-creation path recon is still running in background — I'll fold that
  in when it returns. Meanwhile, next clarifying question:

