  ---
  Design: Tile-clarification redesign

  Section 1 — The new schema (single source of truth)

  Define on the Swift side as @Generable. Cloud (Gemini) gets the JSON-Schema
  equivalent via response_schema. Three layers of defense as both consensus
  models recommended:

  @Generable
  struct Branch {
      @Guide(description: "Short label, 2-4 words")
      var title: String

      @Guide(.anyOf(["concept","action","technical","question","risk"]))
      var type: String

      @Guide(description: "1-5 — how richly this branch could expand",
  .range(1...5))
      var complexity: Int

      @Guide(description: "Should this tile ASK the user a clarifying question 
  instead of expanding into branches? Only true when downstream branches would 
  depend on knowledge ONLY THE USER HAS — preferences, constraints, situation, 
  goals. NEVER true for facts the model could state itself.")
      var shouldAskClarifyingQuestion: Bool

      @Guide(description: "If shouldAsk is true: brief explanation of WHY user 
  input is required for this specific branch. Must reference the user's unique 
  situation, not general knowledge.")
      var clarificationReasoning: String?

      @Guide(.anyOf(["preference","constraint","situation","goal"]))
      var userInputCategory: String?

      @Guide(description: "The clarifying question, only present when shouldAsk 
  is true")
      var clarifyingQuestion: String?
  }

  @Generable
  struct BranchSet {
      @Guide(.count(6))
      var branches: [Branch]
  }

  Why this shape: Forces the model to commit to "ask vs expand" as an explicit
  boolean before naming a category, and makes it justify the ask in
  clarificationReasoning (gemini's flag). All four fields linked: if shouldAsk 
  == false, the other three are nil. The framework enforces enum membership —
  drift is impossible at the grammar level.

  ---
  Section 2 — Few-shot contrastive examples in the prompt

  Both consensus models said this is more reliable than the schema alone. Inject
   3 paired examples after the schema declaration:

  EXAMPLES — when to ASK vs when to EXPAND:

  Root: "cheese", neighbor "storage"
    → shouldAsk: false. Storage methods (refrigeration, wax coating, vacuum
  seal,
      cellar humidity) are facts the model knows. Just expand.

  Root: "fitness app", neighbor "workout plan"
    → shouldAsk: true. category: "goal". reasoning: "branches depend on user's
      fitness goal — weight loss, strength, or endurance produce wildly
      different plans." question: "What's your primary fitness goal?"

  Root: "vacation to Japan", neighbor "itinerary"
    → shouldAsk: true. category: "preference". reasoning: "trip length and
      travel style determine what cities to visit." question: "How many days
      and do you prefer cities or countryside?"

  Root: "JavaScript framework", neighbor "best practices"
    → shouldAsk: false. Best practices (small components, immutability, testing
      pyramid, etc.) are general knowledge. Just expand.

  Why these 4: Two ASK examples (real user-knowledge needed), two EXPAND
  examples that would have triggered false positives under the old prompt.
  Contrastive pairs are how Gemini and FoundationModels learn the rule.

  ---
  Section 3 — Post-hoc validator (codex's third layer)

  After the LLM returns, before clarifyingQuestion becomes a tile, run a
  deterministic check:

  const FACTUAL_LOOKUP_PATTERNS = [
    /^what is\b/i,
    /^what are\b/i,
    /^how do you\b/i,
    /^how does\b/i,
    /^where is\b/i,
    /^how many\b/i,
    /^which (\w+)$/i,  // "which type", "which method"
    /\bdefinition of\b/i,
  ];

  function validateClarification(branch: Branch): Branch {
    if (!branch.shouldAskClarifyingQuestion) return branch;
    if (!branch.clarifyingQuestion) return { ...branch,
  shouldAskClarifyingQuestion: false };

    const isFactual = FACTUAL_LOOKUP_PATTERNS.some(p =>
  p.test(branch.clarifyingQuestion!));
    if (isFactual) {
      // Validator caught a laundered factual question. Drop the ask, let it 
  expand.
      return {
        ...branch,
        shouldAskClarifyingQuestion: false,
        clarifyingQuestion: undefined,
        clarificationReasoning: undefined,
        userInputCategory: undefined,
      };
    }
    return branch;
  }

  Why this matters: Schema enforces shape; validator catches semantic drift.
  Same fallback as not asking — tile expands into branches normally. No UX hole.

  ---
  Section 4 — What gets deleted

  - client/src/pages/HexmindApp.tsx:588-590 — the "MANDATORY FOR 2-3 BRANCHES"
  instruction (whole block)
  - client/src/pages/HexmindApp.tsx:594 — old contextPrompt field in the JSON
  schema string (replaced by the new structured fields)
  - client/src/hooks/useAIGeneration.ts — same prompt cleanup
  - client/src/types/hexmind.ts:317 — rename contextPrompt: string to
  clarifyingQuestion: string for naming consistency (optional)

  What stays:
  - Modal UI for clarification (ContextPromptModal or whatever it's called) —
  unchanged
  - contextHistory map — answered clarifications still propagate as background
  to descendants
  - The trigger logic at line 1220 — just gates on shouldAskClarifyingQuestion
  now

  ---
  Section 5 — What we're explicitly NOT doing

  - No tool-calling. Apple-intelligence skill warned tools 5x token usage (16→79
   for one tool). Worse trade than schema fields.
  - No second LLM call for validation. Adds 2-4s latency on FoundationModels.
  Validator is regex.
  - No lower temperature. Codex suggested it, but our brainstorming use case
  wants creative range (0.7-1.3). Trust the validator instead.
  - No new "ask before root generation" flow. Recon refuted the user's "always
  needs input" claim with caveats — roots already have meaningful seed text from
   one of 5 paths.
  - No visual treatment for "this tile will ask" vs "this tile will expand."
  Polish question; HIG fetch failed; defer until the design ships.

  ---
  Single ask before I write the spec doc: Does this design land, or push back on
   any section?

