❯ /apple-intelligence                                                           

⏺ Skill content already loaded earlier. The sub-skill that's still untapped and
  potentially relevant: app-intents mentions a "Multiple Choice API" —
  System-level pattern where the AI presents a picker instead of text input.

  For our use case that translates to: when the LLM does decide to ask, it emits
   the question plus 3-5 option strings and the user picks one rather than
  typing. Faster, more reliable, and the LLM is much better at generating the
  options than the user is at typing the right answer.

  Folding that into the design as Section 1b — extension to the schema:

  @Generable
  struct Branch {
      // ...same as before...
      var shouldAskClarifyingQuestion: Bool
      var clarifyingQuestion: String?

      @Guide(description: "3-5 likely answers as a tap-to-select picker. The 
  user can still tap 'Other' to type a custom answer. Only present when 
  shouldAsk is true.", .count(3...5))
      var clarifyingOptions: [String]?
  }

  UX change: the existing ContextPromptModal switches from "just a text input"
  to "tap-an-option list with an 'Other...' affordance that reveals the text
  input." The text input still exists as the escape hatch — no capability lost.

  Why this matters more than I thought:
  - LLM strength alignment. Both Apple FoundationModels and Gemini are good at
  generating plausible options for a domain ("What's your fitness goal?" →
  ["Weight loss", "Strength", "Endurance", "General health"]). Cuts user
  friction massively.
  - Validator gets stronger. A clarifying question that only has options like
  "Refrigeration", "Wax coating", "Vacuum seal" is self-evidently a factual
  question pretending to be a user question — the validator can also reject when
   options look like factual answers.
  - Hex aesthetic match. Picker chips on the modal can echo the hex tile shape,
  which keeps it visually coherent without restyling anything outside the modal.

