     Tile-clarification redesign + dynamic-edit polish

     ▎ Plan file. Hexmind iOS / web. Captured 2026-05-09 from /brainstorming →
     ▎ /doubt → /elegance:elegance → /team → /apple-intelligence council with
     ▎ three external/internal critiques (cynic, defiance, conscience) and
     ▎ external consensus (gemini + codex).

     Context

     The bug. When a user enters "cheese" and the LLM generates 6 tiles, one
     might be "storage." Tapping "storage" today opens a modal asking "How
     should you store cheese?" — a factual question the LLM should have just
     answered with branches like "Refrigeration / Wax / Vacuum seal."

     Root cause. The system prompt (HexmindApp.tsx:588-590) instructs the
     LLM:

     ▎ "CONTEXT PROMPTING (CRITICAL - MANDATORY FOR 2-3 BRANCHES): When a
     ▎ concept is too broad/vague to explore meaningfully without specifics,
     ▎ you MUST add a contextPrompt question."

     The MANDATORY count forces the LLM to ask, regardless of whether the
     question is appropriate. The result: clarification fires on factual
     questions, the user can't expand the tile without first answering a
     question they expected the AI to answer.

     Goal. Replace the mandate with a structured rule the LLM can't drift
     from, schema-enforced via Apple's @Generable framework, with a regex
     validator catching semantic drift, and accessibility-correct visual
     signaling of "this tile asks instead of expanding." Fold in user-asked
     dynamism (post-facto edit + regenerate already exist; just need the
     clarifying answer to participate).

     Intended outcome. Tile-tap on "storage" expands into 6 storage
     branches. Tile-tap on "workout plan" (in a fitness root) opens a chip-
     assisted text input asking the user's actual goal. Tiles that ask are
     visually distinct via dashed border + corner badge + "Ask" pill, all
     non-color channels per WCAG 1.4.1. Edits to a tile trigger optional re-
     generation of its neighbors with the new content as context.

     ---
     Part A — Schema + prompt redesign (the core fix)

     A.1 Replace the JSON-mode prompt with @Generable on iOS

     Define on the Swift side at ios/App/App/FoundationModelsPlugin.swift:

     @Generable
     struct Branch {
         @Guide(description: "Short label, 2–4 words")
         var title: String

         @Guide(.anyOf(["concept","action","technical","question","risk"]))
         var type: String

         @Guide(description: "1–5 — how richly this could expand",
     .range(1...5))
         var complexity: Int

         @Guide(description: "Should this tile ASK the user instead of expanding
      into branches? Only true when downstream branches would depend on 
     knowledge ONLY THE USER HAS — preferences, constraints, situation, goals. 
     NEVER true for facts you could state yourself.")
         var shouldAskClarifyingQuestion: Bool

         @Guide(description: "If shouldAsk is true: brief explanation of WHY 
     user input is required. Must reference user's unique situation, not general
      knowledge.")
         var clarificationReasoning: String?

         @Guide(.anyOf(["preference","constraint","situation","goal"]))
         var userInputCategory: String?

         @Guide(description: "The clarifying question, only present when 
     shouldAsk is true")
         var clarifyingQuestion: String?

         @Guide(description: "0–5 short suggested answers (chips). Tapping a 
     chip populates the textbox; user can edit before submitting. Use 0 for 
     purely open-ended questions, 2 for binary-feeling questions, 3–5 for 
     likely-answer accelerators.", .count(0...5))
         var suggestedAnswers: [String]?
     }

     @Generable
     struct BranchSet {
         @Guide(.count(6))
         var branches: [Branch]
     }

     Three layers of defense (per consensus + cynic):

     1. Schema-enforced enum membership (anyOf) — grammar-constrained
     decoding makes invalid values impossible at the token level.
     2. Mandatory clarificationReasoning (gemini's recommendation) —
     forces the model to articulate why user input is needed before it
     can ask.
     3. Few-shot examples in the system prompt — see A.3 below. Both
     consensus models said this is more reliable than the schema alone.

     A.2 Web/Android — Gemini structured output

     Cloud path at client/src/hooks/useAIGeneration.ts:295-525 and
     client/src/pages/HexmindApp.tsx:540-770 already requests JSON mode via
     responseMimeType: "application/json". Upgrade to Gemini's
     response_schema with the equivalent JSON Schema mirroring the Swift
     @Generable shape. Apple FoundationModels and Gemini both use grammar-
     constrained decoding so the constraint is real on both sides.

     A.3 New system prompt (delete old, write fresh)

     Replace HexmindApp.tsx:588-590 and the equivalent block in
     useAIGeneration.ts:

     EXAMPLES — when to ASK vs when to EXPAND:

       Root "cheese" → tile "storage"
         → shouldAsk: false. Storage methods (refrigeration, wax coating,
           vacuum seal, cellar humidity) are facts. Just expand.

       Root "fitness app" → tile "workout plan"
         → shouldAsk: true. category: "goal". reasoning: "branches depend on
           user's fitness goal — weight loss vs strength vs endurance produce
           different plans." question: "What's your primary fitness goal?"
           suggestedAnswers: ["Weight loss", "Strength", "Endurance",
           "General health"]

       Root "vacation to Japan" → tile "itinerary"
         → shouldAsk: true. category: "preference". reasoning: "trip length
           and travel style determine cities to visit." question: "How many
           days, and cities or countryside?" suggestedAnswers: []  (open-ended)

       Root "JavaScript framework" → tile "best practices"
         → shouldAsk: false. Best practices are general knowledge. Just expand.

     Delete entirely: the "MANDATORY FOR 2-3 BRANCHES" block (lines
     588-590 in HexmindApp.tsx). The schema's shouldAskClarifyingQuestion: Bool
     is the only gate.

     A.4 Post-hoc validator (codex's third layer)

     Add client/src/lib/clarificationValidator.ts:

     const FACTUAL_LOOKUP_PATTERNS = [
         /^what is\b/i,
         /^what are\b/i,
         /^how do you\b/i,
         /^how does\b/i,
         /^where is\b/i,
         /^how many\b/i,
         /^which (\w+)$/i,
         /\bdefinition of\b/i,
     ];

     export function validateClarification(branch: Branch): Branch {
         if (!branch.shouldAskClarifyingQuestion) return branch;
         if (!branch.clarifyingQuestion) {
             return { ...branch, shouldAskClarifyingQuestion: false };
         }
         const isFactual = FACTUAL_LOOKUP_PATTERNS.some((p) =>
             p.test(branch.clarifyingQuestion!)
         );
         if (isFactual) {
             return {
                 ...branch,
                 shouldAskClarifyingQuestion: false,
                 clarifyingQuestion: undefined,
                 clarificationReasoning: undefined,
                 userInputCategory: undefined,
                 suggestedAnswers: undefined,
             };
         }
         return branch;
     }

     Call from both call sites that parse the LLM response (HexmindApp.tsx
     local generateNeighbors and useAIGeneration.ts hook). On rejection: same
     fallback as not asking — tile expands into branches normally. No UX
     hole.

     A.5 Type rename for clarity

     In client/src/types/hexmind.ts:317, rename contextPrompt: string →
     clarifyingQuestion: string and add the four new fields. Update all
     ~15 call sites (search-and-replace with manual review for the modal
     binding).

     ---
     Part B — Modal UX: text input + tappable chips (single shape)

     B.1 Reject the multi-input-type schema

     Per defiance's analysis: three classification decisions (ask vs not, what
     category, what input shape) stack errors to ~73% end-to-end if each is
     90%. Yes/No is a trap — it forces lying when the real answer is "kind of"
     or "depends." Collapse to one input shape.

     B.2 The shape

     Existing ContextPromptModal (component in client/src/components/)
     keeps its modal-on-tap firing pattern. UI changes:

     ┌─ "What's your primary fitness goal?" ─────────┐
     │                                                │
     │  [ Weight loss ]  [ Strength ]                 │  ← chips (if
     suggestedAnswers)
     │  [ Endurance  ]  [ General health ]            │
     │                                                │
     │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐      │
     │  │ Type or tap a chip to start...       │      │  ← textbox (always
     present)
     │  └──────────────────────────────────────┘      │
     │                                                │
     │  [ Skip ]                       [ Submit ]     │
     └────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     Chips populate the textbox; do NOT submit it. User can tap "Weight
     loss" then edit it to "Weight loss but slow and sustainable." If
     suggestedAnswers is empty, only the textbox renders. Same component,
     two states — no new components.

     B.3 Behavior

     - Tile tap with shouldAskClarifyingQuestion === true → modal opens
     with clarifyingQuestion as title, suggestedAnswers as chips,
     textbox empty.
     - User taps a chip → textbox value = chip label. Cursor at end. Chip
     visual goes selected.
     - User edits textbox → chip selection clears (text no longer matches).
     - Submit → answer goes to contextHistory[nodeKey], then
     generateNeighbors(node, false, answer) fires.
     - Skip → generateNeighbors(node, false, "") fires (no context).
     - Already-answered tile → modal does NOT re-open on subsequent taps.
     Instead, expand-into-branches behavior. The answer is preserved in
     contextHistory and stays available for descendants.

     ---
     Part C — Visual signaling (NON-color, per WCAG)

     C.1 Reject full-background-color approach

     Per conscience's WCAG analysis: solid background colors from
     NODE_TYPES.bgSolid fail 1.4.3 contrast on 3+ of 6 palette colors
     with no text color that passes AA across the palette (yellow at 1.07:1,
     cyan at 1.94:1, amber at 2.15:1 against white). The user's intuition that
     "colors are more accessible" is the opposite of true here — the existing
     6-type palette already saturates the color channel, and adding a 7th
     meaning to color forces the contrast failures.

     C.2 Three non-color channels (the minimum for WCAG 1.4.1)

     Modify client/src/components/HexCanvas.tsx (tile rendering, lines
     250-267) to layer these signals when branch.shouldAskClarifyingQuestion ===
      true:

     1. Dashed border — stroke-dasharray="4 3" on the hex SVG path.
     Geometric, survives all color-blindness simulators.
     2. Corner badge — HelpCircle icon (already imported from
     lucide-react) in the top-right of the hex, 16×16, with
     bg-popover + text-popover-foreground for owned contrast.
     3. Visible "Ask" pill — small text label, 8–10px, mirroring the
     existing cluster badge pattern at HexCanvas.tsx:364.

     Optional 4th (gated on prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference):

     4. Slow pulse on the badge only — never the whole tile. 2s
     duration. Disabled when reduce-motion is set.

     C.3 nodeTypes.ts addition

     Add to client/src/lib/nodeTypes.ts:

     export const ASK_INDICATOR = {
         borderDash: "4 3",
         badgeIcon: HelpCircle,
         badgeBg: "bg-popover",
         badgeFg: "text-popover-foreground",
         pillText: "Ask",
     };

     C.4 Already-answered state

     When contextHistory[nodeKey] has a value, the tile reverts to the
     normal expand visual (solid border, no badge, no pill). VoiceOver gets
     the "answered" label per C.5. This signals "you already gave context;
     this will expand now."

     C.5 Screen reader strings

     Per conscience's table, in HexCanvas.tsx at the aria-label site
     (line 176):

     ┌───────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
     │         State         │                   aria-label                    │
     ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Normal expand tile    │ "{node.text}, {type.label}"                     │
     ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Ask tile              │ "{node.text}, {type.label}, needs               │
     │ (un-answered)         │ clarification"                                  │
     ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Ask tile (answered)   │ "{node.text}, {type.label}, answered"           │
     ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Root                  │ "{node.text}, core node"                        │
     └───────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     Modal must add role="dialog", aria-modal="true", aria-labelledby
     pointing at the question heading, and return focus to the originating
     tile button on close. Badge icon must be aria-hidden="true" — the
     parent button's aria-label carries the meaning.

     ---
     Part D — Dynamic editing + regenerate-on-edit

     User wants this "very dynamic." The recon found EditModal.tsx already
     supports full post-creation editing (title, description, type). The
     missing piece: when a tile is edited, downstream neighbors don't
     regenerate automatically.

     D.1 Add an optional regenerate-on-edit affordance

     In client/src/components/EditModal.tsx, extend the existing onSave
     callback to accept regenerateNeighbors: boolean. UI: a checkbox at the
     bottom of the modal: [ ] Re-generate this tile's branches with the new 
     content.

     D.2 Wire to the existing regeneration paths

     Recon mapped three paths:
     - refreshSingleNode (HexmindApp.tsx:875-990) — keeps position, regen
     title/description/type.
     - generateNeighbors (lines 540-872) — regen all 6 neighbors.
     - Force-on-tap (lines 1215-1231) — auto-fire when neighbors empty.

     D.1's "regenerate neighbors with new content" hooks into path 2 with
     forceRefresh = true. No new code path, just a new caller.

     D.3 Persist clarification context across regenerations

     Currently contextHistory is local React state and is NOT propagated
     into node data. If a tile regenerates, the clarification answer is still
     in contextHistory[nodeKey] and gets re-injected via the
     additionalContext parameter — verified at HexmindApp.tsx:603 and 1822.

     No change needed for clarification persistence — it already survives
     edits and regenerations. Just document the invariant in a code comment.

     ---
     Part E — What we're explicitly NOT doing

     - No tool-calling. Apple-intelligence skill: tool definitions ~5×
     token usage (16 → 79 for one tool). Schema fields are cheaper.
     - No second LLM validation call. Adds 2–4s latency on
     FoundationModels. Regex validator runs in <1ms.
     - No yes/no input type. Defiance's strongest objection — yes/no
     hides third options and forces lying. Chips with ["Yes", "No"] cover
     this case if the LLM emits them.
     - No new "ask before root generation" flow. Root recon refuted the
     user's "always needs input" claim with caveats — roots already have
     meaningful seed text from one of 5 paths (3 from user, 2 from
     templates with their own seed).
     - No full-background-color signal. WCAG 1.4.3 blocks it. Use the
     three non-color channels in Part C instead.
     - No lower temperature. Codex suggested it; rejected because our
     brainstorming use case wants creative range (0.7–1.3). Trust the
     validator.
     - No context propagation to descendants. Currently scoped to direct
     generation; promoting to descendants is a separate decision with
     prompt-bloat tradeoffs.

     ---
     Critical files

     Modify:
     - ios/App/App/FoundationModelsPlugin.swift — add @Generable Branch +
     BranchSet, switch from text response to respond(generating:).
     - client/src/lib/foundationModelsPlugin.ts — JS-side type contract
     must match the Swift schema.
     - client/src/hooks/useAIGeneration.ts (lines 295-525) — system prompt
     rewrite, schema validator wiring, response parsing.
     - client/src/pages/HexmindApp.tsx (lines 540-770, 875-990, 1215-1231,
     1700-1840) — local generateNeighbors prompt rewrite, refresh wiring,
     modal binding, edit-with-regenerate option.
     - client/src/components/SettingsModal.tsx — N/A (no settings change).
     - client/src/components/EditModal.tsx — add the "regenerate neighbors"
     checkbox.
     - client/src/components/ContextPromptModal.tsx (search for the actual
     filename) — add chip strip above textbox, chip-populates-textbox
     behavior, no chip-submits-on-tap.
     - client/src/components/HexCanvas.tsx (lines 250-267 for path
     rendering, 176 for aria-label, 364 for cluster badge pattern) — add
     dashed-border + corner badge + "Ask" pill rendering when
     shouldAsk === true.
     - client/src/lib/nodeTypes.ts — add ASK_INDICATOR token.
     - client/src/types/hexmind.ts (line 317) — rename contextPrompt →
     clarifyingQuestion, add 4 new fields.

     Add new:
     - client/src/lib/clarificationValidator.ts — the regex validator from
     Part A.4.

     Reuse (don't reinvent):
     - EditModal already does title/description/type editing.
     - refreshSingleNode (HexmindApp.tsx:875-990) for any tile-content
     regeneration.
     - generateNeighbors(node, forceRefresh=true) for neighbor
     regeneration after edits.
     - NODE_TYPES palette and style.icon/style.color/style.border
     pattern at HexCanvas.tsx:142-152.
     - cluster badge rendering pattern at HexCanvas.tsx:364 — mirror
     exactly for the "Ask" pill.
     - lucide-react HelpCircle (already imported somewhere in the
     codebase per conscience's check) for the corner badge.
     - contextHistory map and additionalContext parameter — already
     survives edits and regenerations.

     ---
     Verification

     End-to-end test, manual on simulator + on web:

     1. The reported bug — Enter "cheese" as root. Tap any tile labeled
     storage / preservation / refrigeration. Should expand into branches,
     not open a modal. Repeat 5×; modal should never fire on factual
     tiles.
     2. Legitimate clarification — Enter "fitness app". One of the 6
     tiles should ask a real user-knowledge question (likely "What's your
     fitness goal?" or similar). Tap it. Modal should open with the
     question, 3–5 chips above a textbox.
     3. Chip behavior — In the modal, tap a chip. Textbox should populate
     with chip text. Edit the textbox. Chip selection should clear. Submit.
     The next generation should reflect the answer.
     4. Already-answered tile — Tap the same clarification tile again
     after submitting. Should expand into branches (no modal). VoiceOver
     should announce "answered."
     5. Validator catches drift — Force a factual question through the
     schema (set shouldAsk=true + clarifyingQuestion="What is pasteurization?"
     in a test). The validator should drop it; tile
     should expand into branches instead.
     6. Visual signal — On a healthy ask tile, verify dashed border +
     corner badge + "Ask" pill all render. Disable color in macOS
     Accessibility Inspector — tile should still be distinguishable from
     normal tiles.
     7. VoiceOver — Enable VoiceOver. Walk through 6 tiles where one is
     an ask tile. Confirm announcement matches the table in C.5.
     8. Edit + regenerate — Edit a tile's title from "storage" to "long-
     term aging." Tick the "re-generate this tile's branches" checkbox.
     Save. The 6 neighbors should re-generate based on "long-term aging."
     9. Run automated tests — pnpm test (vitest) — 68 should pass.
     pnpm check — type check should pass with new schema fields. pnpm build && 
     npx cap sync ios — clean.
     10. Token budget — In Xcode console, log
     model.tokenUsage(for: instructions) and for: prompt —
     confirm the new prompt + few-shot examples don't exceed ~25% of the
     4096-token context window. Apple-intelligence skill notes that tool
     definitions inflate tokens 5×; we're avoiding tools, so budget should
     be comfortable.

     Estimated change size

     ~250-350 lines across 10 files, plus ~80 lines new in
     clarificationValidator.ts, minus ~50 lines deleted from the old
     prompt + old contextPrompt string-based flow. Net additive, but
     mostly because the schema declaration is verbose. Behavioral surface is
     SIMPLER — one input shape, one validator, one "should-ask" decision.

     Risks

     1. @Generable in Capacitor. Our Swift plugin currently returns raw
     text via session.respond(to:). Switching to session.respond(to:, 
     generating: BranchSet.self) returns typed Swift values; we need to
     serialize back to JSON for the bridge call. This is straightforward
     with JSONEncoder but needs care that the field names match what the
     JS side expects.
     2. Gemini schema mismatch. Cloud responses must match the Swift-
     side @Generable shape exactly. A drift means the JS parser fails
     on cloud but works on device (or vice versa). Mitigation: extract a
     single TypeScript type that's the source of truth and match both
     schemas to it.
     3. Validator over-rejects. Some legitimate questions start with
     "What" ("What matters most to you?"). The current regex only matches
     ^what is\b and ^what are\b, not all "what" questions, so this
     should be fine — but worth a manual sweep through 10 generation runs
     to confirm.
     4. Schema enforcement on Gemini. Both consensus models confirmed
     response_schema IS grammar-constrained (not just a hint), but
     Gemini's docs are less explicit than Apple's. Worth a quick test
     with a deliberately invalid value to confirm it gets rejected at the
     API level vs returned-and-validated client-side.

