⏺ Good check — let me verify I didn't re-introduce cloud on iOS.

  Searched for 2 patterns (ctrl+o to expand)
⏺ Yes, iOS is still 100% Apple Foundation Models. No regression.

  The Gemini responseSchema I added gets built into the requestPayload object,
  but requestPayload is only ever sent to /api/generate on web/Android — the
  iOS-only gate from the earlier multi-provider strip (commit cfabcfe) is intact
   and untouched:

  HexmindApp.tsx:
  - Line 715: if (!preFetchedBranches && !isIos()) — cloud-warning toast skipped
   on iOS
  - Line 724: if (isIos() && !preFetchedBranches) — early return, never reaches
  the fetch at line 757
  - Line 1023: refresh path same pattern — iOS short-circuit

  useAIGeneration.ts:
  - Line 506: if (isIos()) early return — fetch at line 535 never reached on iOS

  What each platform sees today:

  Platform: iOS
  Inference: Apple FoundationModels (only)
  Schema enforcement: Prompt-only (Part A.1 / @Generable not yet landed)
  Validator: ✅ regex catches drift
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Platform: Web
  Inference: Gemini cloud via /api/generate
  Schema enforcement: ✅ Full responseSchema (this session's A.2)
  Validator: ✅ same regex
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Platform: Android
  Inference: Gemma local → cloud fallback (untouched)
  Schema enforcement: ✅ responseSchema on cloud leg
  Validator: ✅ same regex

  Net for iOS: the new prompt + validator + UI improvements (chips, ask styling,
   edit-cascade) all apply. The model-side enforcement is the prompt + Apple's
  own JSON-mode adherence + the post-hoc validator. Without @Generable, the LLM
  can drift on the new enum fields — but the validator catches the most common
  drift (factual questions laundered as user-knowledge) before any UI state
  updates.

  The Gemini responseSchema is dead code on iOS. It's built into the payload
  object but never serialized to a network request. Costs nothing at runtime;
  harmless. Could trim with another if (!isIos()) on the schema field but not
  worth it.

  If you want full grammar enforcement on iOS too, that's exactly what Part A.1
  (Swift @Generable BranchSet) would deliver. Same hard guarantees as Gemini's
  responseSchema, but at the token level on Apple's neural engine.

  Want me to do A.1 next, or hold and let you test the current state first?

