✻ Cogitated for 25s

❯ thinking about load flow. Why don't we: drop user into the prompt entry on an 
   empty grid, as we currently do. Have them enter a prompt, wait until the     
  initial tiles generate around it, and then dim the screen again and give the  
  walkthrough tour. Near the end we can mention the model will decide whether   
  some things are interesting enough to expand on their own; and ideally I'd    
  like to record gifs or similar of the actual interactions to show there. This 
   gives them immediate engagement in the app with lwo time to value without    
  leaving them stranded. Meanwhile, the map should be collapsible in some       
  manner to free up another upper right UX element, and we don't need the       
  fullscreen option                                                             

⏺ Good redesign instinct — the current "tour-on-cold-start with empty grid"
  loses people because there's nothing to point at yet. Let me recon the
  existing onboarding + tour + minimap code in parallel, then propose the
  design.

  Searched for 1 pattern, read 1 file (ctrl+o to expand)

⏺ Good news — the existing OnboardingTour already waits until nodeCount > 1
  before showing the cards. But your ask is sharper: wait until generation FULLY
   completes (not just first neighbor arrives), then DIM the screen, THEN show.
  Let me see the minimap + right cluster.

  Read 2 files (ctrl+o to expand)

⏺ Got the full picture. Five changes needed:

  1. Tour deferral: wait for !isGenerating before showing (currently triggers
  when nodeCount > 1)
  2. Tour dim: backdrop overlay during tutorial
  3. Tour: 5th step on auto-expand + media slot for GIFs
  4. Minimap collapsible: inline collapse control replaces the toolbar toggle
  5. Drop fullscreen icon + the now-unused minimap toggle from the right cluster

  Starting with the OnboardingTour rewrite (biggest piece).

